(module crux-shape-probe-row-lax)
;; THE PER-ROW CHECK DROPPED, on stage H's probe manifest. The envelope clauses
;; are kept, array and non-empty, and the conjunct requiring every row to carry
;; the file and mutant_file keys is dropped. A manifest whose rows are
;; arbitrary passes as long as it is a non-empty list.
;;
;; WHY THIS IS THE FAILURE THAT MATTERS AND NOT A TIDINESS CHECK. The reference
;; indexes `p["file"]` and `p["mutant_file"]` UNGUARDED immediately after this
;; validation, so a row missing either key is a KeyError and a traceback at
;; exit 1 rather than a decision. The port has no host exception to fall
;; through, so the guard is what turns that crash into a recorded
;; failed/Errored, on the precedent proposal section 9.1 item 1 set for the
;; unguarded reads in stages B and I. Dropping the conjunct here does not make
;; the port lenient, it makes the port index a key that is not there.
;;
;; The pairing matters as much as the clause. This crux and
;; crux-probe-polarity-inverted sit on opposite sides of stage H's declared
;; write: this one feeds the PRE-write halt, which records `failed`, and that
;; one feeds the POST-write bar, which records `stopped` via the reference's
;; only require_written. One stage, two halts, two dispositions, two cruxes.
;;
;; DISCRIMINATION, measured at v0.14.87 over this body:
;;
;;   [H7-ARRAY] + [H7-NONEMPTY] + [H7-NO-HARDCODE]  -> SAFE
;;   [H7-ROWS] alone                                -> REFUTED
;;
;; [H7-NO-HARDCODE] survives for the same reason it does in
;; crux-shape-accepts-malformed: this body is strictly WEAKER than the honest
;; decision, so the converse implication still holds and an anti-hardcoding
;; post cannot catch a validator that accepts too much.
(def probe-rows-conform? [is-array: bool
                          row-count: int
                          rows-conforming: int] -> bool

  (pre (and (>= row-count 0)
            (and (>= rows-conforming 0)
                 (<= rows-conforming row-count))))

  (post (=> result is-array))
  (post (=> result (> row-count 0)))
  (post (=> result (= rows-conforming row-count)))

  (post (=> (and is-array
                 (and (> row-count 0)
                      (= rows-conforming row-count))) result))

  (and is-array (> row-count 0)))
